structured_output.content:
The bug was in sqlparse/filters/reindent.py's DML-splitting logic (used to insert newlines before certain keywords, e.g. before a DML statement start). It checked `if prev_:` to decide whether to insert a newline before the token — but `prev_` had just been deleted (whitespace) so it could be a leftover reference. The real question is "is this the first token in the statement", not "was there a previous token" (which could reference a since-deleted node). For 'select foo;
select bar;', after StripWhitespaceFilter, the second statement starts with an empty Newline token followed by 'select'; `token_prev` skipping ws=False returned that empty-value whitespace token as `prev_`, which is truthy (it's a token object, not None), even though it's effectively nothing. This caused a newline to be inserted when it shouldn't have been at certain positions, or the reverse — extra blank line only for some separator forms (e.g. `select foo;
select bar;`) but not others (e.g. `select foo; select bar`), which is exactly the inconsistency reported (the assertion `'select foo; ...' == 'select foo; ...'` failing only for the "stmts" test list that includes forms with embedded newlines/semicolons before the second SELECT).

Fix applied: after deleting the previous whitespace token when present, check `tidx > 0` (i.e., whether there is still a preceding token in the list) rather than the truthiness of the old `prev_` reference, to decide if a newline should be inserted before the keyword. This makes all the semicolon/statement-separator variants produce the same normalized output ('select foo;

select bar' family) instead of some getting an extra spurious blank line.

Verified with manual repro across multiple statement-separator styles (`select foo; select bar`, `select foo
; select bar`, `select foo; -- test
select bar`, `select foo;
select bar;`, `select foo;select bar;`) — all now consistently reindent with exactly one blank line between statements, and the previously-passing subquery/UNION reindent case is unaffected.

